<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n64232" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λῡσῐῳδός</span></b> , ὁ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>one who played women's characters
				in male attire</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Aristox.
				<u>Fr.Hist.</u>
				57
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Posidon.
				4
			</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				J.
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Phld.
				<u>Ind.Sto.</u>
				7
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Plu.
				<u>Sull.</u>
				36
			</span></i>: so called from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Λῦσις</span>, who
			wrote songs for such actors, <i><span class="en">
				Str.
				14.1.41
			</span></i>: distd. from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μαγῳδός</span>,
			ibid., but identified with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μ.</span> by
				<i><span class="en">
				Aristocl.Hist.
				7
			</span></i>: fem. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἡ λυσιῳδός</span>
			Ath.Naucr. (<i><span class="en">
				<u>FGrH</u>
				166
			</span></i>) <i><span class="en">
				1
			</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				J.
			</span></i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> as Adj.,
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λ. αὐλοί</span> flutes <i>that
				accompanied such songs</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ephor.
				3
			</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				J.
			</span></i> (nisi leg. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λυσιῳδ&lt;ικ&gt;<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ῶν</span></span>).</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
